# How this course was made (W3C PROV-O): written from the sources below,
# then checked in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-dcat-3/> ,
              <https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-dcat-2/> ,
              <https://www.w3.org/TR/turtle/> ,
              <https://semiceu.github.io/DCAT-AP/releases/3.0.1/> ,
              <https://semiceu.github.io/DCAT-AP/releases/3.0.0-hvd/> ,
              <https://github.com/SEMICeu/DCAT-AP/tree/4470b8ea907b7eb9ec55ab22befd1ecd2210ff32> ,
              <https://github.com/DIGGSweden/DCAT-AP-SE/tree/6b5124b8cb480a62b834f19abffa3de41b09dfb5> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the course was planned by AI agents from research notes on DCAT 3, DCAT-AP 3.0.1 and DCAT-AP-SE 3.0.1, read from local copies of the specifications and their repositories; three outlines were drafted from different angles (the specifications' own order, a publisher describing one dataset, and the portals that search and harvest catalogs) and merged into one, and the chapters were then written from it in English and translated from the English into Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French. The working notes, the outline and the glossaries are kept with the course's sources, not in the library."@en ,
                 "The course was checked by machine (the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators, its course and Markdown checks, a Turtle parser for every example, and a second SHACL validator) and by AI agents against the sources; it has had no human review."@en ,
                 "Selection: what a learner needs to read and write DCAT metadata and to make it conform to DCAT-AP: just enough RDF and Turtle; DCAT 3's model, its classes and the properties a dataset, a distribution, a data service and a catalog are described with; responsibility, licenses and rights; series, versions, related resources and identifiers; then DCAT-AP's conformance rules for senders and receivers, its mandatory, recommended and optional properties class by class, its controlled vocabularies, SHACL validation, harvesting and high-value datasets; and a closing chapter on what DCAT-AP-SE leaves out, tightens and adds, DIGG's recommendations and harvesting to Sveriges dataportal. The agencies and datasets in the scenarios and examples are invented, and their addresses end in .example. Left out on purpose: points the sources leave unclear or contradict (such as the Dataset Series' superclass in DCAT-AP), the status labels of the specifications' pages, and section numbers and exact counts as things to learn."@en ,
                 "Structure: 10 chapters of 36 steps in all. Each step is a short theory text in Markdown, read in two chunks, often with a short Turtle example, and two or three multiple-choice questions on it; each chapter has review questions that combine its steps and are asked only in its final review (four in the first chapter, two in every other): 106 step questions and 22 review questions, 128 questions in all. Every question has one correct option (the card's back) and three wrong options (its distractors) of similar length and form, each with a note on why it is wrong. Every text is in English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French; the Turtle examples are the same in every language."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the course is CC0 1.0, like most of the library. Its theory, questions, options, notes and examples are in the course's own words. DCAT 3 is under the W3C Software and Document License, which allows copying and changing with its notice; the course quotes only short definitions and usage notes from it, attributed in the text. DCAT-AP and DCAT-AP-SE are CC BY 4.0: their facts are restated (changed) and translated in the course's own words, with a few short phrases quoted and attributed, and they are credited in the sources below. RDF 1.1 Turtle was used for facts only."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the whole release, with the library checker (npm run library:check), which validates it against Solid Memo's shapes (library deck format 6, card format 5, chapter, step and distractor format 1), DCAT-AP and SKOS, with the reference data, checks the course's outline by its rules and its Markdown by the rules for a release, and with a second, independent SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck) over the same shapes; every Turtle example in the theory parsed with the N3 parser and compared, byte for byte, across the six languages."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the release conforms to every shape, profile and rule the checker applies to it, the crosscheck agrees, and all 36 Turtle examples parse and are the same in every language."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Outline: facts and question design (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: each chapter of the merged outline checked by two AI agents, one that tried to refute every teaching point, answer and wrong option against the specifications, and one for the questions' design (one answer, recall from the front alone, options of the same form); then the outline read as a whole by a third, for facts taught twice, contradictions, forward references and gaps; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 195 findings, 193 fixed in the outline before the chapters were written and 2 rejected with a reason (both superseded by other fixes); among them requirement levels misstated, a property put on the wrong class, DCAT and DCAT-AP rules blurred, wrong options that were defensible, fronts that gave the answer away, and backs that stood out by their length."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter text in English: facts, design and language (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: each chapter as written, reviewed by three AI agents: for every fact, quotation, Turtle example, answer and wrong option against the specifications; for the questions' design, each front read alone first; and for the English, the Markdown and the release's format rules; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 240 findings across the ten chapters, all fixed in the text but 9, which were rejected with a reason after checking the sources; among them claims that went further than the specifications, a quotation the sources do not contain, an HVD property called mandatory where the example showed another, an invented agency's dates and formats that contradicted each other within a chapter, a question that had a second correct answer when studied from its front alone, and terms used before they were taught."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Course as a whole in English (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the ten chapters read together by two AI agents, one for facts taught twice, contradictions, names and IRIs used differently and forward references, one for every question's options (backs that are the longest or stand out by form, and fronts that do not work as flip cards); not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 29 findings, all fixed (one in part); among them one example IRI typed as a dataset in one chapter and as a series in another, a forward reference to a later chapter, and backs shortened or wrong options lengthened so that the back does not stand out."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-27T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Translations into Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: for each language, a glossary of the course's terms drawn up first by an AI agent (in Swedish from DCAT-AP-SE's own labels, with DIGG's Swedish wording wherever the English quotes it); each chapter translated from the English by an AI agent, then reviewed by two more, one for fidelity, translating every text back into English and comparing it with the English, and one as a native editor for natural language, terminology and typography; then each language's ten chapters read together by an AI agent for consistency; the title, description and keywords translated and checked by translating them back into English; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 732 findings across the 50 chapter translations (Swedish 137, Korean 137, German 172, Spanish 132, French 154), fixed in the text or, where fidelity to the English won over style, rejected with a reason; the consistency pass then evened out terms and voice within each language."@en .

# The sources (W3C PROV-O): what the course took from each, and on what license.
<https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-dcat-3/>
    dcterms:title "Data Catalog Vocabulary (DCAT) – Version 3" ;
    dcterms:creator "W3C" ;
    dcterms:license <https://www.w3.org/copyright/software-license-2023/> ;
    rdfs:comment "The page's header: \"Copyright © 2024 World Wide Web Consortium. W3C® liability, trademark and permissive document license rules apply.\" The permissive licence is the W3C Software and Document License (https://www.w3.org/copyright/software-license-2023/): \"Permission to copy, modify, and distribute this work, with or without modification, for any purpose and without fee or royalty is hereby granted\", provided its notice is included \"on ALL copies of the work or portions thereof\". Content taken: the course explains DCAT 3 in its own words, with short definitions and usage notes quoted and attributed in the text; its Turtle examples are the course's own. Reliability: normative (W3C Recommendation of 22 August 2024). Read in October 2026. Used for: every fact about DCAT 3 in chapters 1 to 7: its purpose, namespaces and conformance, its classes and properties and their usage notes, dataset series, versions, qualified relations, inverse properties and identifiers, and what changed since DCAT 2."@en .

